WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.120
INTRO:
For decades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:06.400
large companies have been trying to dominate the&nbsp;
online video calling and messaging market. We’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:11.600
seen everything from WebEx, MSN messenger,&nbsp;
and Skype to Google Hangouts and Zoom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:16.640
but it seems like people’s favorite doesn’t&nbsp;
come from any of these large companies. Rather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:23.520
their top choice is often Discord. This is quite&nbsp;
ironic given that projects like Google and Skype&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:29.600
have billions of dollars behind them and thousands&nbsp;
of employees. After all, Microsoft literally spent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:36.800
$8.5 billion just to acquire Skype. So, clearly,&nbsp;
they take this sector quite seriously. Yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:41.040
they’re getting crushed by a relatively&nbsp;
small startup that was founded by a gamer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:46.640
Skype does technically have more active users&nbsp;
currently clocking in at 300 million while Discord&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:52.720
only has about half of that at 150 million. But&nbsp;
when you consider that Skype was launched in 2003&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:58.560
and that Discord wasn’t launched till 2015, it&nbsp;
becomes clear who’s really winning the user race.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:02.480
Not to mention, a lot of Skype’s user base&nbsp;
comes from companies forcing their employees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:07.200
to use Skype while virtually everyone who&nbsp;
uses Discord chooses it out of free will.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:11.840
So, here’s how a gamer showed up big tech in one&nbsp;
of the most saturated industries in the world.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:17.120
JASON CITRON:&nbsp;

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:21.760
Taking a look back, the story of Discord dates&nbsp;
back to a man named Jason Citron who was born on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:27.680
September 21, 1984 in Florida. Jason’s interest&nbsp;
in technology and especially gaming started very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:32.480
early on in his life thanks to his father and&nbsp;
childhood friends. Apparently, Jason’s father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:36.960
was a massive fan of technology himself and he was&nbsp;
always tinkering with personal computers in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:42.400
free time. At the time, personal computers weren’t&nbsp;
actually all that useful to ordinary people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:47.440
given that their capabilities were extremely&nbsp;
limited and Windows 3.1 hadn’t even come out yet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:52.560
Nonetheless, being a businessman, Jason’s father&nbsp;
could see that PCs were gonna become huge hence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:58.240
the constant tinkering with PCs. While Jason’s&nbsp;
father’s curiosity no doubt rubbed off on Jason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:02.720
Jason says that a lot of his passion for tech&nbsp;
comes from gaming and it was actually a childhood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:08.160
friend that introduced him to games. Apparently,&nbsp;
one of his childhood friends owned an NES and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:12.320
Jason had the chance to play Super Mario Bros&nbsp;
on it. Jason describes this experience as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:17.200
quote “the most amazing thing I’d ever&nbsp;
seen in my entire life.” As he grew older,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:22.320
his love for gaming evolved from being a player to&nbsp;
being a developer. Jason took his first computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:26.800
science class in middle school and became friends&nbsp;
with another coding enthusiast within the class.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:32.080
Soon after, Jason hosted a sleepover for his 13th&nbsp;
birthday, and once everyone else went to sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:36.480
Jason and his comp sci friend spent the night&nbsp;
trying to code a role playing game using QBASIC.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:41.440
Given that this was their first try, the&nbsp;
game probably wasn’t that great; however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:45.840
Jason says that this experience made it clear to&nbsp;
him that his career had to be related to gaming.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:50.480
Moving forward, through the rest of his teenage&nbsp;
years, Jason continued to be an avid gamer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:54.320
Some of his favorite games include Super&nbsp;
Metroid, Chrono Trigger, Earthbound,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.320 --> 00:03:00.880
Uniracers, Street Fighter, Mortal Kombat, and most&nbsp;
importantly Final Fantasy VI. Aside from gaming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:04.880
Jason started to use his knowledge of computers&nbsp;
to start earning income as a teenager.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:09.600
Jason says that he quickly realized that most&nbsp;
people were relatively clueless about computers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:13.680
which gave him an opportunity to help them&nbsp;
fix computer problems and earn some money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:18.320
Jason’s father was especially supportive of&nbsp;
this endeavor and would regularly drive him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:21.760
all around the neighborhood, so that he&nbsp;
could make this side hustle a reality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:26.160
As Jason slowly built up a reputation as being a&nbsp;
computer prodigy, people started to ask him to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:30.800
bigger tasks such as writing code for them. In&nbsp;
fact, this side hustle grew so big that Jason's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:35.680
parents helped him set up a bank account and Jason&nbsp;
started receiving wire transfers from customers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:40.400
If your customers are using wire transfers to&nbsp;
pay you, you were clearly making big money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:44.400
Despite this, Jason didn’t instantly delve&nbsp;
into starting a full on development company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:48.560
He says that he was intimidated of hiring people,&nbsp;
so he preferred to keep his side hustle a solo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:53.680
venture. But, with Jason’s ambitions, it was just&nbsp;
a matter of time until his projects outgrew him.

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:57.680
MOBILE DEVELOPER:&nbsp;

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:01.680
Moving into college, Jason attended Full&nbsp;
Sail University in Florida presumably to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:06.160
major in computer science. Given that Jason had&nbsp;
been coding for several years at this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:11.040
college wasn’t that difficult for him and he&nbsp;
would end up graduating early. After graduation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:16.240
Jason moved to San Rafael, California and picked&nbsp;
up a job as a console game developer. Initially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:21.600
he got himself his own place and lived alone, but&nbsp;
he says that this got boring pretty quickly. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:25.520
he moved into a college house and became&nbsp;
roommates with 9 UC Berkeley students.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:31.200
While this may seem suboptimal at first glance,&nbsp;
this actually turned out to be extremely valuable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:36.320
You see, about 6 months after moving into&nbsp;
the house, a man named Rohan Relan moved in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:40.560
Like Jason, Rohan was a software enthusiast&nbsp;
and his uncle and he were actually planning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:45.040
on launching an incubator called YouWeb that was&nbsp;
supposed to help mobile games get off the ground.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:49.520
As you would guess, Jason was extremely intrigued&nbsp;
by this idea, and Rohan would eventually invite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:54.480
Jason to join the incubator. At the incubator,&nbsp;
Jason would meet another developer named Danielle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:59.120
Cassley and together, they would develop their&nbsp;
first major mobile game called Aurora Feint.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:03.120
The game combined two of Jason's favorite&nbsp;
genres which were role playing games and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:07.760
puzzle games. So, Jason was basically living&nbsp;
his dream being able to work on developing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:14.160
game that really interested him as a career. All&nbsp;
of this took place in late 2007 and early 2008,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:18.480
so Apple had just released the iPhone and&nbsp;
the app store wasn’t even a thing yet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:23.280
Jason and Danielle weren’t exactly developing the&nbsp;
game specifically for the iPhone, but once the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:27.440
app store was launched, it was clear to them&nbsp;
that they had to address the iPhone market.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:31.680
This was actually perfect timing because it&nbsp;
allowed Aurora Feint to be one of the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:36.800
games on the app store. And given that most of the&nbsp;
original apps on the app store weren’t that great,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:42.800
Aurora Feint was quickly able to build up a solid&nbsp;
user base of over 1 million users. Despite this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:47.280
the game wasn’t very profitable given that&nbsp;
there was no recurring revenue from customers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:52.480
Apple didn’t even support in-app purchases at the&nbsp;
time, so Jason and Danielle only earned money on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:58.240
the upfront cost of the game which was $1. At one&nbsp;
point, Jason only had two months worth of funding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:02.080
left, and he remembers calling his parents&nbsp;
and telling them that he might need to move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:06.560
in with them. But, luckily for Jason, that’s&nbsp;
when he would come up with his best idea yet.

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:10.640
OPENFEINT:&nbsp;

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:14.800
Jason’s breakthrough moment came when he was&nbsp;
having a conversation with Rohan’s uncle, Peter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:19.440
about where to take Aurora Feint. During this&nbsp;
conversation, Jason said that it was weird that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:24.720
XBox live wasn’t available on iPhone. Given&nbsp;
how fast the mobile gaming market was growing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:29.280
you would think that either Microsoft or Apple&nbsp;
would’ve released something similar to XBox live,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:34.400
but this wasn’t the case. And this is when Rohan’s&nbsp;
uncle realized that Aurora Feint already had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:39.200
aspects of XBox live within it. So, it wouldn’t&nbsp;
be that difficult to create their own version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:44.480
XBox live called OpenFeint. Jason didn’t have any&nbsp;
money left though, so he pitched this idea to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:48.320
few investors who agreed to fund the app if he&nbsp;
could prove that game developers would support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:53.600
the venture. Later that week, Jason posted the&nbsp;
idea of OpenFeint on TechCrunch which resulted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:57.760
in hundreds of game developers signing up and&nbsp;
pleading their support. And with that Jason was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:04.000
funded and he would code like crazy over the&nbsp;
next several weeks. And on February 17, 2009,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:08.560
Jason would launch OpenFeint. Given that there no&nbsp;
native alternative and the overwhelming support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:13.840
from game developers, OpenFeint quickly caught on&nbsp;
amongst mobile gamers. OpenFeint’s user base grew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:17.680
into the tens of millions which allowed Jason&nbsp;
to complete funding rounds that each raised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:22.560
millions. It looked like things couldn’t get any&nbsp;
better, and that’s exactly when Apple launched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:26.720
Game Center. Jason said that he felt like the&nbsp;
air was being sucked out of his lungs as he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:32.400
realized that he was now competing against Apple.&nbsp;
Despite Jason’s fears, Game Center actually ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:37.840
up being a massive propellant for OpenFeint. You&nbsp;
see, Jason integrated OpenFeint into Game Center&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:42.400
and integrated Game Center into OpenFeint.&nbsp;
So, OpenFeint users could enjoy the benefits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:47.120
of OpenFeint while new Game Center users could&nbsp;
naturally discover OpenFeint. It was a win-win&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:52.480
scenario, and at the peak of OpenFeint’s success,&nbsp;
they boasted 120 million users and supported 7000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:58.080
games. Given this popularity, I don’t think you’d&nbsp;
be too surprised to hear that GREE would go on to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:03.360
buy OpenFeint for $104 million. It’s not clear&nbsp;
what percentage of OpenFeint Jason owned before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:07.600
the acquisition, but I think it’s safe to assume&nbsp;
that this sale made Jason a multi millionaire at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:12.800
the very least if not a deca millionaire. While&nbsp;
Jason has a pretty nice ending, the same cannot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:17.680
be said about OpenFeint. Around the same time,&nbsp;
a coalition of gamers would sue OpenFeint for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:22.880
violating their privacy, and a little over a year&nbsp;
later, GREE would announce that they’re shutting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:27.360
down OpenFeint due to overwhelming backlash.&nbsp;
And that was the bitter end of OpenFeint.

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:32.080
DISCORD:
While losing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:36.880
OpenFeint was likely a massive blow for&nbsp;
Jason, he was pretty well off financially.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:42.320
So he had the luxury of taking some time off&nbsp;
and focusing on something else. Initially, Jason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:47.440
actually turned to creating another gaming company&nbsp;
called Hammer &amp; Chisel in 2012. This company would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:51.840
release a mobile game called Fates Forever,&nbsp;
but this never really garnered much attention.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:55.760
Disappointed by the launch, Jason often spent&nbsp;
a lot of his time playing games like Final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.760 --> 00:09:00.160
Fantasy XIV and League of Legends. One of&nbsp;
the first things that stuck out to him as he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:04.720
played these games was that their in game voice&nbsp;
communication services were absolute garbage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:08.480
Soon after, Jason set out to develop a user&nbsp;
friendly voice communication service that had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:13.840
minimal impact on game performance, and that’s&nbsp;
how Discord was founded. Over the next year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:18.640
Jason worked with YouWeb to develop Discord, and&nbsp;
given that he’d been a gamer for his entire life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:24.320
he knew exactly what gamers wanted. Jason would go&nbsp;
on to launch Discord in May of 2015, and he says&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:28.960
that he didn’t market it to any specific audience.&nbsp;
But naturally, one of the first communities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:33.360
that started embracing the platform were gaming&nbsp;
subreddits. This quickly made it popular amongst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:38.080
Twitch streamers and eventually eSports and LAN&nbsp;
tournament gamers. One of the first companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:43.440
that saw the potential of Discord was Time Warner&nbsp;
Cable who invested into the company in late 2016.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:48.160
Two years later, Microsoft would also embrace the&nbsp;
company by bringing Discord support to Xbox live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:53.840
users. Microsoft would actually eventually try&nbsp;
to buy Discord for $12 billion, but Jason would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:59.200
turn down this offer in April of 2021. And given&nbsp;
that Discord has since successfully completed a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:04.000
funding round at a $15 billion valuation, it looks&nbsp;
like turning down the acquisition deal was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:09.520
right decision. But, it’s not all sunshines and&nbsp;
rainbows ahead for Discord. Given that Discord&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:14.800
doesn’t directly charge any of its users, they’re&nbsp;
revenue per user is likely not that great. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:19.360
considering how they’re constantly completing new&nbsp;
funding rounds, it’s likely that Discord is losing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:24.880
money. This is fine for a startup, but you can&nbsp;
only keep up a money losing company for so long.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:30.960
So, Discord needs to focus on monetization&nbsp;
and profitability ASAP, but I think Jason is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:35.840
well aware of that, and that’s exactly what he’s&nbsp;
trying to do. So, over the next couple of years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:40.880
it’s likely that Discord only grows even bigger&nbsp;
and potentially even becomes the world’s largest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:46.000
voice communication platform, but we’ll just have&nbsp;
to wait and see. What’s your favorite aspect about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:50.880
Discord? Comment that down below. Also, drop&nbsp;
a like if you think that Discord blows out the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:55.120
competition. And of course consider checking out&nbsp;
our international channels to watch our videos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.120 --> 00:10:58.880
in other languages and consider subscribing&nbsp;
to see more questions logically answered.

